# The Gupta Age: India's Classical Golden Age

> From about 319 to 550 CE the Guptas reunited the Ganga plain and set off
> an extraordinary run of mathematics, poetry, medicine, and temple art —
> Aryabhata's zero, Kalidasa's dramas, the Iron Pillar, and Nalanda all
> belong to this classical moment. Harsha's brief empire and southern
> Pallava-Chalukya art carry the story into the seventh century.

## 1. Rise of the Guptas

**Chandragupta I** (319-335 CE) took the title *maharajadhiraja*, married
the **Lichchhavi** princess Kumaradevi, and launched the **Gupta era** with
his coronation year **319-320 CE**.

@diagram tree id="gupta-rulers" layout="tree" aria="The main Gupta rulers from Chandragupta I to Skandagupta"
  Guptas -> "Chandragupta I (319-335 CE)"
  Guptas -> "Samudragupta (335-375 CE)"
  Guptas -> "Chandragupta II (375-415 CE)"
  Guptas -> "Kumaragupta I (415-455 CE)"
  Guptas -> "Skandagupta (455-467 CE)"
@/diagram

**Samudragupta** (335-375 CE) is known almost entirely from the **Allahabad
(Prayag) Prashasti**, a eulogy in Sanskrit verse by his court poet
**Harisena**. It lists the uprooted kings of Aryavarta, the twelve
Dakshinapatha rulers reinstated as vassals, and frontier kings paying
homage. He performed the **ashvamedha** — his gold coins show the sacrifice
horse — and V. A. Smith dubbed him the **"Indian Napoleon."**

## 2. Chandragupta II Vikramaditya and the Zenith

Chandragupta II destroyed western **Shaka** power (Ujjain became his second
capital), took the title **Vikramaditya**, and hosted the Chinese pilgrim
**Fa-Hien** (c. 399-414 CE). His court's **Navaratna** ("nine gems")
included **Kalidasa** and the lexicographer **Amarasimha**.

| Ruler | Title / marker | Key facts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chandragupta I | Maharajadhiraja | Gupta era begins 319-320 CE |
| Samudragupta | Kaviraja | Prayag Prashasti; ashvamedha |
| Chandragupta II | Vikramaditya | Shakas destroyed; Fa-Hien visits |
| Kumaragupta I | Mahendraditya | Founded Nalanda mahavihara |
| Skandagupta | Vikramaditya II | Defeated Hunas; repaired Sudarshana lake |

## 3. A Golden Age? Achievements

- **Aryabhata** (b. 476 CE): the *Aryabhatiya* explains place value with
  **zero**, states that the **earth rotates**, gives pi as 3.1416, and
  explains eclipses as shadows — not demons.
- **Varahamihira**: the *Brihatsamhita*, an encyclopaedia of astronomy and
  omens.
- **Medicine:** the *Charaka Samhita* (medicine) and *Sushruta Samhita*
  (surgery — 121 instruments, cataract and nose operations) attained
  classical form.
- **Kalidasa:** *Abhijnanashakuntalam*, *Meghaduta*, *Raghuvamsha*. Shudraka
  wrote *Mrichchhakatika*; Vishakhadatta the *Mudrarakshaka*; the
  *Panchatantra* was compiled.
- **Metallurgy:** the **Delhi Iron Pillar** (Mehrauli) of Chandragupta II
  has resisted rust for over 1,500 years.
- **Art:** the Sarnath Buddha, the Bhitarargaon brick temple, the
  Dashavatara temple at Deogarh, and the **Ajanta** frescoes.

[info title="Nalanda"]
Kumaragupta I founded the **Nalanda** mahavihara; by Xuanzang's day it held
thousands of students and a nine-storey library, teaching grammar, logic,
medicine, and Buddhist philosophy to scholars from Korea to Central Asia.
[/info]

## 4. Administration and Economy

The Gupta state was **decentralised** compared with the Mauryas: land
grants to Brahmanas (**agrahara**) and officials, hereditary officials, and
guilds (**shreni**) issuing coins. Gold **dinara** coins of remarkable
beauty circulated; urbanisation slowed in the late phase and Roman trade
declined, but Southeast Asian trade grew — Sanskrit inscriptions appear in
**Funan** (Cambodia) and Champa, carrying Indian scripts, temples, and law.

| Mauryan feature | Gupta reality |
| --- | --- |
| Paid bureaucracy | Land grants to office-holders |
| Central spy network | Feudatories: parivrajaka, Uccakalpa, Maitrakas |
| State coinage | Guptas and guilds both mint |

[warning]
"Golden age" needs qualification: Fa-Hien notes growing **untouchability**
(chandalas announcing themselves with wood clappers in the street), and
the earliest epigraphic hints of temple women (**devadasis**) come from
late-classical inscriptions of the fifth-sixth centuries.
[/warning]

## 5. Fall of the Guptas

**Skandagupta** (455-467 CE) repelled the first **Huna** wave — his Junagadh
inscription records repairing the Mauryan Sudarshana lake — but the invasions
of **Toramana** and his ferocious son **Mihirakula** broke the western
empire. Weak successors, the drift to land grants, and the loss of western
ports reduced the Guptas to a Magadha rump by about **550 CE**.

## 6. Harsha of Thanesar (606-647 CE)

Harsha of the **Pushyabhuti** house rebuilt a north Indian empire from
Thanesar to Kanauj. His court poet **Banabhatta** wrote the *Harshacharita*;
the Chinese pilgrim **Xuanzang** stayed 630-644 CE, studied at Nalanda, and
praised Harsha's generosity at the great **Prayag assemblies**, where the
king gave away his treasury every five years. His southward march was
halted on the Narmada by the Chalukya **Pulakeshin II** (Aihole
inscription). After Harsha died in 647 without an heir, his minister
Arjunashva misgoverned briefly into Chinese-Tibetan intervention.

## 7. The Classical South: Pallavas and Chalukyas

| Dynasty | Capital | Signature works |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Pallavas | Kanchipuram; port Mamallapuram | Rathas and Shore Temple, Kailasanatha temple |
| Chalukyas of Badami | Vatapi (Badami) | Aihole temples, Pattadakal, Badami caves |

The **Aihole inscription of Ravikirti** (634-635 CE) praises Pulakeshin II
defeating Harsha. Pallava-Chalukya rivalry (Pulakeshin II vs.
Mahendravarman and Narasimhavarman I, who captured Vatapi) drove
architectural experiment that seeded the Dravida temple style.

## Key Takeaways

- The Gupta era (from 319 CE) delivered classical peaks: Aryabhata's
  mathematics, Kalidasa's poetry, Sushruta's surgery, Nalanda, and the
  rust-free Iron Pillar.
- The Allahabad Prashasti of Harisena is the key source for Samudragupta's
  conquests; Fa-Hien and Xuanzang give the Chinese view of Gupta and
  Harsha's India.
- Gupta rule was grant-based and decentralised — effective while rich,
  brittle before the Hunas (Toramana, Mihirakula).
- Harsha (606-647) was the classical north's last great emperor; the
  Pallava-Chalukya duel shaped southern temple art.
